WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.536 --> 00:00:10.490
MARKUS KLUTE: All right, hi.

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.650
So we continue the
discussion in 8.701 on units.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.370
So this unit is on units.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.110
Why this is an
important discussion

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.400
is because it is very convenient
and it simplifies life

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.200
quite a bit if one does not
use SI units in the discussion

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.560
of, in particle physics,
decays in particle physics,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.690
or cross-sections.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.030
And it's also important
to avoid carrying

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.942
around large exponents.

00:00:37.942 --> 00:00:39.400
And given, you have
a conversation,

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.858
you want to talk
about things which

00:00:40.858 --> 00:00:48.690
are order of 1 of things instead
of 10 to the minus 28 things.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.180
And one example is
the introduction

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.780
of a new unit for the
cross-sections of-- units

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.780
for cross-sections
which describes an area,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.590
and that unit is barn.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.130
We talk about
cross-sections of barns,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:06.120
or femtobarns, or
picobarns, and one bar

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.620
is defined as 10 to the
minus 28 square meters.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.950
Physics processes, the one at
high energies-- for example,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.620
the ones we discuss at the
Large Hadron Collider--

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.700
are typically of the
order of picobarn,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:25.120
10 to the minus 12 barns, or one
femtobarn, 10 to the minus 15.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.080
So there's an interesting
story to barns

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.210
and why this was introduced.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.450
The unit came out of
the Manhattan Project.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:39.450
The idea of the scientist was
to confuse potential spies

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.620
towards what cross-sections
for nuclear processes are.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.620
And so they introduced
this unit of a barn,

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.280
and they're trying
to characterize

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.180
nuclear collisions, maybe an
accelerator shooting something

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.200
at a target.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.570
And one barn is a
cross-section where

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.350
it's really, really hard to
miss-- so a big cross-section.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.590
In this context, also,
the shed was introduced.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.150
This is not very
popular today anymore.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.830
And it turns out that
this idea of confusing

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.630
the readers of papers
or of discussions

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.340
turned into a new standard.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.350
So we talk about,
of course, barns,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:23.080
and picobarns and femtobarns,
specifically, quite frequently.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.310
So this is just one example.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.893
This is not really
changing the units,

00:02:25.893 --> 00:02:30.300
but just avoiding
carrying around exponents.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.030
But we also, in particle
physics and in nuclear physics,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.090
use a system called
natural units.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.150
This system is based
on fundamental concepts

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.040
of quantum mechanics
and special relativity.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.600
So the idea here is that we
replace kilogram, meters,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.890
and seconds by h-bar, which
is a unit of action in quantum

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.500
mechanics, c, the
speed of light,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:58.430
and GeV, where
GeV, a typical GeV,

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.480
is a typical approximate
mass of a proton.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.150
So then, you do
this transformation,

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:10.850
and then you simplify the option
of setting h-bar and c to 1,

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:14.390
you find that energies
are expressed in GeV,

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:19.100
momentum is expressed in GeV,
and mass is expressed in GeV.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.775
That means when you talk
about relativistic equations,

00:03:22.775 --> 00:03:28.370
E equals m c squared and all
those things, m, E, and also

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.920
the momentum have the same unit.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.320
That simplifies quite a bit.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.260
Time has a unit of
1 over GeV, length

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.890
has a unit of 1 over GeV,
and area has the unit of 1

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:43.180
over GeV squared.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.930
So that's the simplification.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.870
You might think that
you lose information

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.900
by setting fundamental
constants to 1,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.670
but you actually
do not, because you

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:55.390
carry with you,
in your equations,

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.870
the dimension of the problem.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.360
If you want to do a
quick exercise here,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.870
I invite you to
calculate the charge

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:14.570
radius of the proton, which
is 4.1 over GeV, or per GeV,

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:18.140
and convert this
back to SI units.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.420
Again, it seems like we
lost information here,

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.160
but just from the
dimensional analysis,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.000
you can figure out
what the answer is.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.780
And the hint here
is that h-bar c is

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:32.238
equal to 0.197 GeV femtometers.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.780
So you should already
know the answer

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.640
from previous discussions
in the lecture,

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:39.750
but the calculation is
rather straightforward.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:44.970
On top of this, it's useful
to use Heaviside-Lorentz units

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.560
and combine them with some
measurable units we discussed.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.990
So what we do in addition
here is set, say, permittivity

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:58.080
in free space to 1, and also
the permeability in free space

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.710
to 1-- so epsilon0 and mu0 to 1.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:06.150
When you do that, you
basically combine or tie

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:11.160
the electric charge to
the strength of QCD.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.580
And so alpha, the strength, a
dimensionless fine structure

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:22.330
constant, 1 over 137, becomes
e squared, the electric charge

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.480
squared, over 4 pi.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.610
So this is also very
convenient, to have

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.940
this kind of convention.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.000
So we'll use those natural
units as we go through class.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.590
In some examples, we'll use SI,
in others, use natural units.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:42.050
This will always be clear from
the problem we're looking at.

